<PageLayout title="Bring the story into focus" eyebrow="The Arahkaii partner desk" dek="A practical guide to interviews, evidence, images and the first conversation." description="How to prepare an Arahkaii story pitch, founder interview, studio visit or brand feature. What to send, image credits and what happens after publication.">
	<p>You do not need all of this to <a href="/partnerships/#brief">send a first pitch</a>. Start with a short introduction. If the story is selected, this guide helps us make the reporting useful.</p>
	<h2>Find one thing worth understanding</h2>
	<p>Choose a concrete decision, material, process, place or problem. Explain what changed, who was involved and what a reader could learn. A useful starting question: “What would someone miss if they only saw our finished product?”</p>
	<h2>For an interview</h2>
	<ul><li>The interviewee’s name, role and relationship to the work.</li><li>Three specific things they can speak about from experience.</li><li>A choice that did not work, a trade-off or an unexpected result.</li><li>Dates, names and primary sources for claims we should verify.</li><li>Preferred format, availability and any embargo to discuss before sharing restricted material.</li></ul>
	<p>We can begin in writing. A follow-up conversation or visit may be needed. Responses should come from the person being quoted; a publicist can coordinate, but should identify who supplied each answer.</p>
	<h2>For a visit or demonstration</h2>
	<p>Tell us the location, what can be observed, who can explain it, accessibility arrangements and any photography restrictions. Disclose supplied products, hospitality, travel or other support. Access does not guarantee favourable coverage.</p>
	<h2>For images</h2>
	<p>At the pitch stage, a link to a small selection or a press kit is enough. If selected, we’ll ask for original files and written permission for the agreed uses.</p>
	<ul><li>A horizontal lead image, ideally at least 2,400 pixels wide.</li><li>A portrait of the person we interview, where relevant.</li><li>Process, material or place details that help explain the story.</li><li>For each image: filename, subject, photographer, copyright holder, caption and restrictions.</li><li>Confirmation covering use on the article and any separately agreed social promotion.</li></ul>
	<p>Owning a brand does not necessarily mean owning the photographer’s rights. We confirm permissions before publication. Conceptual or AI-generated images must be identified and cannot stand in for documentary evidence.</p>
	<h2>For a paid proposal</h2>
	<p>Include your objective, intended reader, central idea, timing, indicative budget and any required usage rights. We scope creative work, reporting, deliverables, approvals, labelling and measurement before either side commits. Newsletter placements depend on the service being active and an audience we can verify.</p>
	<h2>When a feature is commissioned</h2>
	<p>Agree the launch handover early: a canonical article link, approved excerpt, accurate credits and any commissioned social assets. A brand may share a link to its published feature; republishing the full article, using Arahkaii’s logo in advertising or implying an endorsement requires a separate agreement.</p>
	<p>If useful, agree a later check-in with new evidence. Reporting may cover a changed collection, an updated technique or what the founder learned next. Avoid promising search results, sales or permanent placement.</p>
	<p><a class="tlink" href="/partnerships/#brief">Send your first brief →</a></p>
</PageLayout>
